<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Zeelandia (Guyana)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Zeelandia is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> brick fort on Fort Island in the mouth of the Essequibo River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>. Built in the 18th century, it replaced the older stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyk-over-al">Kyk-over-al</a> as the seat of the Dutch colony of Essequibo, housing the commander and the colony's governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/court-of-policy">Court of Policy</a>. The fort and the nearby Court of Policy hall survive and are kept as heritage sites.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-guyana.jpg" alt="Fort Zeelandia (Guyana)" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. van Henemann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vlaggeneiland-Fort-Zeelandia-Essequebo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fort Island is a fluvial island of the Essequibo River delta, in the Essequibo Islands-West Demerara region, and the fort stood strategically at the mouth of the river. It is a small structure of 15 by 20 m with ramparts at its four corners, not to be confused with Fort Zeelandia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>. Its lozenge-shaped design, submitted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurens-storm-van-s-gravesande">Laurens Storm van 's Gravesande</a>, resembles forts built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in the same period. The lower of its two storeys served as a warehouse and a safe powder house, the upper housed the soldiers, and each storey had twenty gunports for 2- or 3-pounder guns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the beginning of the 18th century the commanders of Essequibo recommended moving the colony's administrative centre, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers had moved to the fertile banks of the river. In 1726 it was resolved to build a fort to protect the planters and the interests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, and an engineer, Leslorant, was sent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to build a horn work with a wooden redoubt and a palisade on the northern point of Vlaggeneiland (Flag Island). In 1739 the seat of the colony moved there from Fort Kyk-Over-Al.</p><p>In August 1738 Storm van 's Gravesande, then secretary to Commander Gleskerk, reported that the fort was falling to pieces and recommended one of brick. Construction began in 1740 and, with the labour of enslaved Africans, the fort was completed in 1743, though shortages of materials and labour delayed the whole complex until 1749. Brick was baked on the spot, mortar and trass came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the fort was named after the County of Zeeland, home of many of the first settlers.</p><p>By 1781, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a>, the fort was unfit for use. When six British privateers arrived in late February, the Council instructed its commander, Captain Severyn, to fire a blank shot and then a pro-forma shot and surrender; the privateers seized 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> vessels, and two sloops sent by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-brydges-rodney-1st-baron-rodney">Lord Rodney</a> accepted the colony's surrender. The French occupied the island the next year, the Dutch regained it two years later, and from 1796 the fort declined as attention shifted to Demerara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fort is among the oldest structures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> and has been designated a National Monument by the National Trust of Guyana. On 15 November 1995 it was added, with the Court of Policy Building, to the UNESCO World Heritage Tentative List in the cultural category.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Zeelandia (Guyana)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The seat of government moved to <b>Fort Zeelandia</b> on Fort Island in the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/essequibo">Essequibo</a></li></ul></section>
